window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-22","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-22-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-01/","title_he":"א – איסור דם","title_en":"The Prohibition of Blood","body_en":"<p>Together with the permission to eat meat not in the framework of a sacrifice, we were forbidden to eat the blood, as it is stated (Devarim 12:20-23): \"When the Lord your God shall enlarge your border, as He has promised you, and you shall say: I will eat meat… then you shall slaughter (tishḥat) of your herd and of your flock, which the Lord has given you, as I have commanded you, and you shall eat within your gates, after all the desire of your soul… Only be steadfast not to eat the blood, for the blood is the life (nefesh), and you shall not eat the life with the meat…\" That is to say, blood has a special purpose – to sustain the life of the animal – and therefore, even though the Torah permitted us to eat meat, it did not permit eating the blood.</p><p>There are two kinds of blood: 'dam ha-nefesh' (life-blood) and the rest of the blood. 'Dam ha-nefesh' is the blood that gushes forcefully after the sheḥita, with which the soul departs; one who eats it intentionally is liable to karet, whereas one who eats of the rest of the blood is liable to lashes (Keritot 20b; SA 67:1). In 'dam ha-nefesh' the vitality of the animal is more prominent, and therefore when a sacrifice is offered, it is from this that one sprinkles on the altar in order to atone for the soul. It turns out that one who eats it also harms the honour of Heaven, for he eats the blood that was fit to be sprinkled on the altar. As it is stated in the section dealing with sacrifices (Vayikra 17:10-11): \"And whatever man there be of the house of Israel, or of the strangers who sojourn among them, who eats any manner of blood, I will set My face against that soul that eats blood, and will cut it off from among its people. For the life of the flesh is in the blood, and I have given it to you upon the altar to make atonement for your souls; for it is the blood that makes atonement by reason of the life.\"</p><p>Since blood is meant to sustain the life of animals and not for human consumption, the Rishonim wrote that one who eats blood is liable to acquire beastly traits and the trait of cruelty in his soul (Ramban; Ḥinukh 148).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The prohibition of blood applies both to the impure species and to the pure species whose meat is forbidden because of nevela or tereifa, and one who eats their blood transgresses two prohibitions: one – the general prohibition that applies to them, and the second – the prohibition of blood. As will be explained in the next halakha, the less developed species, such as fish and locusts, have no prohibition of blood, and if they are impure fish and locusts, one who eats their blood transgresses the single prohibition of eating impure species (Keritot 21a).<br>The prohibition of 'dam ha-nefesh' is similar to the prohibition of ḥelev, since both are fit for the altar and the punishment for both is karet (above 21:1, n. 1). However, there is a difference between blood and ḥelev: ḥelev is forbidden only in species of domestic animal that are fit for a sacrifice, and not in pure species of wild animal, such as the deer and the hart, which are fit to be eaten but disqualified for a sacrifice. Likewise, there is no prohibition of ḥelev in impure species that are forbidden to be eaten. But as for the prohibition of blood, its basis is that it is meant to sustain life, and consequently it is forbidden to eat the blood of all animals, even impure ones (Mishna Ḥullin 117a; SA 66:1). And since this is so, one who eats their 'dam ha-nefesh', with which their soul departs, is liable to karet.<br>It is explained in the Gemara, Keritot 4b, that three times in the Torah it is stated that for blood with which the soul departs one is punished with karet: 1) Vayikra 7:27: \"Whatever soul it is that eats any blood, that soul shall be cut off from its people.\" 2) Ibid. 17:10: \"And whatever man there be of the house of Israel, or of the strangers who sojourn among them, who eats any manner of blood, I will set My face against that soul that eats blood, and will cut it off from among its people.\" 3) Ibid. 17:14: \"For the life of all flesh is its blood; whoever eats it shall be cut off.\" One is for the blood of a sacrifice, the second for the blood of ḥullin (non-sacred animals), and the third for the blood of a wild animal and a bird, that even though it was covered with earth as the Torah commands, the punishment for eating it is karet. Five more times it is stated that it is forbidden to eat blood: 1) Vayikra 3:17: \"It shall be a perpetual statute throughout your generations in all your dwellings, that you shall eat neither fat nor blood.\" 2) Ibid. 7:26: \"And you shall eat no manner of blood, whether of fowl or of beast, in any of your dwellings.\" 3) Ibid. 17:14: \"For as for the life of all flesh, its blood is with its life; therefore I said to the children of Israel: You shall eat the blood of no manner of flesh.\"<br>4) Devarim 12:16: \"Only you shall not eat the blood; you shall pour it out upon the earth like water.\" 5) Ibid. 12:23-24: \"Only be steadfast not to eat the blood, for the blood is the life, and you shall not eat the life with the meat. You shall not eat it; you shall pour it out upon the earth like water.\" Three of them are a warning for the three kinds of blood with which the soul departs, whose punishment is karet. The fourth is for dam evarim (blood of the limbs), that is, blood that was absorbed in the limbs and came out of them. The fifth is for 'dam ha-tamtzit' (draining blood), the blood that comes out of the place of the sheḥita after the soul has already departed. It is worth adding that one of the meanings of the Torah's warning to be steadfast not to eat the blood is that one must take pains to kasher the meat in order to remove the blood from it (Rashbam; Rabbi Yosef Bekhor Shor)."}]},{"slug":"17-22-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-02/","title_he":"ב – גדרי איסור דם","title_en":"The Parameters of the Prohibition of Blood","body_en":"<p>The prohibition of blood applies to the developed species: domestic animals, wild animals and birds, as it is stated (Vayikra 7:26): \"And you shall eat no manner of blood, whether of fowl or of beast (behema), in any of your dwellings\"; the wild animal is included in \"beast\" (Keritot 21b). However, for the less developed species, such as fish and locusts, there is no prohibition of blood. That is to say, the prohibition of blood applies to the species that require sheḥita, and not to the species that do not require sheḥita. As we learned (above 18:11), one of the reasons for the mitzva of sheḥita is to minimise the suffering of the species whose meat we eat, and the mitzva applies to species whose brain and nervous system are more developed and which therefore also feel more pain. But the less developed species, such as fish and locusts, feel less pain, and therefore there is no obligation to slaughter them and no prohibition to eat their blood.</p><p>Blood that is absorbed in the meat in its natural way is permitted to be eaten. Therefore, it is permitted to eat a piece of raw meat. However, since when the meat is cut blood may ooze out at the place of the cut, one must rinse the place of the cut, and only afterwards is it permitted to eat it raw without further kashering (SA 67:1-2).<sup>2</sup></p><p>When meat that has not been kashered is cooked, all of it becomes forbidden because of the blood that comes out of it during cooking and is absorbed in it again. And if this piece was cooked within a dish, since we have no ability to estimate how much blood is in it, we are stringent to regard all of it as blood, and as long as the dish does not contain sixty times its volume, the entire dish is forbidden (SA 69:11).<sup>3</sup></p><p>Therefore, one must kasher meat before cooking it. However, according to the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim, kashering meat is meant to prevent a rabbinic prohibition, since blood that has been cooked or salted is forbidden only by the words of the Sages, because it has changed from its nature and is not fit to be sprinkled on the altar.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"It is explained in Ḥullin 15b and Shabbat 128a that raw meat is permitted to be eaten without salting. And this is the opinion of the great majority of Rishonim, among them Tosafot, Rosh, Rashba and Ran. However, according to the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 6:12), it is forbidden to eat a piece of raw meat without salting. And they explained that in his opinion only swallowing raw meat is permitted, but when one eats it, while chewing it the blood separates from the meat and becomes forbidden (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 67:9; Yad Yehuda, Arokh 5). In any case, all agree that it is forbidden to grind raw meat and eat it, since the blood in it separates from within the meat. And salting will not help it either, since one would have to salt every crumb on all its sides. The only way to kasher meat that has been ground is by roasting (Yabi'a Omer 5, YD 5)."},{"n":3,"en":"There is a fundamental dispute as to the law in a case where the blood flows within the meat and does not separate out. According to Tosafot (Ḥullin 14a, s.v. 've-nisbin'), the Rosh, the Ra'ah, the Ran and SA 69:11, there is no prohibition in it. And according to the Rashba, Rabbeinu Peretz and the Rema, even though the blood did not separate from the meat, since after the sheḥita it moved from its place and shifted within the meat, the blood and the meat with it become forbidden; and the proof is from an animal whose neck was broken after the sheḥita, whose meat it is forbidden to eat raw (Ḥullin 113a), because the blood that was stirred to come out of it as a result of the sheḥita goes back. And the lenient opinions hold that when the neck is broken a special accumulation of blood is created, as in a blow where blood has collected, and therefore the blood and the meat become forbidden. (See below, note 9.)"},{"n":4,"en":"According to the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim, blood that has been cooked or salted is forbidden rabbinically; and so wrote Tosafot, the Teruma, the Yere'im, the Roke'aḥ, the Ra'avan, the Or Zarua, the Rashba, the Rosh, the Ra'ah, the Mordekhai, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, the Ritva and the Ran. And so wrote the Beit Yosef, and the Rema in Darkhei Moshe 69:19. And likewise among the Aḥaronim: Shakh 70:49; Taz; Pri Ḥadash 69:1; Rabbi David Pardo; Noda Bi-Yehuda; Beit Yehuda; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 67:5-6. On the other hand, according to Rashi, the Ittur, Rabbeinu Yehonatan of Lunel and the Rambam, blood that was cooked is forbidden by Torah law. And so wrote the Kanfei Yona, the Erekh Ha-shulḥan and the Ḥatam Sofer. Thus, seemingly, in their opinion salting and roasting are meant to prevent a Torah prohibition. However, the Nekudot Ha-kesef (87:5) wrote that even in their opinion, only blood in its own right, such as blood collected in a vessel, that was cooked or salted is forbidden by Torah law. But blood that was absorbed in its natural way in the limbs and came out of the meat through cooking or salting involves no Torah prohibition. Consequently, it follows that even according to their view, salting and roasting are meant to prevent a rabbinic prohibition. And so wrote Responsa Rim of Gur (YD 7) and the Maharsham (3:320)."}]},{"slug":"17-22-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-03/","title_he":"ג – דרכי הכשרת הבשר מדם","title_en":"Methods of Kashering Meat from Blood","body_en":"<p>There are four methods of kashering meat from the blood in it: 1) salting,<br>2) roasting, 3) scalding in boiling water, 4) scalding in wine vinegar.</p><p>Salting and roasting are meant to draw the blood out of the meat. Salting does so by means of the salt, which draws the blood out of the meat. Likewise, roasting draws the liquids and the blood out of the meat and burns them. And indeed, even after salting or roasting has been completed, a red liquid still remains in the meat; however, according to halakha there is no prohibition in it, and it is not considered blood but 'ḥemar basar' (meat juice, literally \"meat wine\"). Moreover, even if we say that the salting or roasting did not succeed in drawing all the blood out of the meat, as long as the salting or roasting was carried out according to the law, there is no longer any prohibition on the red fluid that remains in the meat, and it is permitted to cook it, since it is impossible that the prohibition of blood should apply to blood that people cannot remove from the meat, for the Torah was not given to the ministering angels. In addition, according to the overwhelming majority of poskim, blood that has been salted or cooked is forbidden only by the words of the Sages, and they ruled that after proper salting or roasting, there is no longer any need to be concerned about the blood remaining in the meat, and if it flows out, it is permitted to eat it (Ran).<sup>5</sup></p><p>In contrast to salting and roasting, which draw the blood out of the meat, scalding in boiling water or in vinegar is not meant to remove the blood from the meat but to seal it into the meat, so that the blood absorbed in the meat will no longer be able to move from its place even during cooking. And since there is no prohibition in blood that is absorbed in the meat, after scalding it is permitted to cook the meat.</p><p>However, the Geonim forbade kashering meat by scalding, lest the meat not be scalded properly. For scalding in boiling water depends on their succeeding in sealing the blood into the meat with a strong blast of heat; but if the water is not boiling enough, or if the meat is not put into the water all at once, or if the boiling water is a small quantity relative to the meat, it will not have the power to seal the blood into the meat. Likewise, regarding scalding in vinegar, there is a concern that the vinegar will not be strong enough for this. There is also a concern lest the vinegar turn slightly red, which is a sign that the scalding did not succeed and the meat and the vinegar have become forbidden, and perhaps they will not notice this (Ḥullin 111a; SA 67:5; 73:2). Therefore, one who wishes to cook meat must first kasher it by salting or roasting.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"According to the Rambam and the Ra'ah, salting draws the blood out of the outer part of the meat only, and in order that the blood inside should not come out, one must scald the meat in boiling water, whereby the blood will be sealed into the meat and will not separate, and consequently there will be no prohibition in it. However, according to most poskim, even though salting and roasting cannot remove all the red liquids in the meat, there is no need to scald the meat. And so is the halakha, as will be explained below (halakha 7, n. 12). And seemingly this is difficult, since even after salting blood still remains in the meat. However, in truth it is difficult to define what blood is, since blood contains red and white cells; presumably the red ones are treated as blood, but they too are not in a fixed state, since salting and roasting can empty some of the liquids in them, and the question is how to define what remains. Indeed, according to the Rashba, kashering meat by salting or roasting removes all the blood in it, and the red liquids that remain in it are not blood but fluid (mohal). And so wrote the Teruma and the Ḥinukh. And according to most poskim, who did not write as the Rashba did, one must say that even if the chemical composition of the fluid that comes out of the meat after salting and roasting is like that of blood, halakhically it does not have the status of blood. For what the Sages forbade – eating cooked blood – refers to blood that can be removed from the meat by salting or roasting; but liquids that cannot be removed by salting or roasting are not considered halakhically as blood, but as fluid and \"ḥemar basar\". And as we learned in the previous note, according to the overwhelming majority of poskim, blood that was cooked with the meat is forbidden only rabbinically. And even according to those who hold that blood absorbed in meat that was cooked is forbidden by Torah law, it is impossible that this prohibition should apply to blood that people cannot remove from the meat, for the Torah was not given to the ministering angels. Therefore we are compelled to say that the prohibition of blood does not apply to the red liquids that remain in the meat after salting and roasting, and even if it flows out, there is no prohibition in eating it."},{"n":6,"en":"The Geonim wrote regarding scalding in boiling water and in vinegar: \"For nowadays we are not expert in scalding, and a doubtful prohibition is treated stringently… and so is the custom throughout Israel\" (Rif, Ḥullin 39b). Apparently, when Israel dwelt together and the size of the vessels and the manner of dividing the meat into pieces were known, the tradition of scalding was clear. However, once they were exiled from place to place, the vessels and the size of the pieces changed, and consequently concerns arose regarding the quantity of water and its heat and the size of the cuts of meat. Likewise regarding wine vinegar, the tradition was lost of how much vinegar is required for what size of piece of meat; and perhaps there are places where the grapes are different and the wine vinegar is weak; and perhaps they did not know which redness constitutes a sign of prohibition.<br>After the fact, if meat was already scalded in boiling water or vinegar and afterwards cooked without salting, the meat is kosher, since the Geonim did not intend to forbid it after the fact (Rashba; SA 73:2; Shakh; Pri Ḥadash; and many others). True, some hold that even after the fact meat that was scalded is forbidden, lest it not have been scalded properly (Baḥ; Ḥavat Da'at 9; Darkhei Teshuva 69:330). However, the halakha follows the lenient opinion, which is the majority, and the dispute concerns a rabbinic law, since cooked blood is forbidden by the words of the Sages. Not only that, but the Geonim wrote that when it is not possible to salt or roast, one may cut the meat into small pieces and throw them one by one into boiling water that gives off a great deal of steam, while making sure that the water continues to boil and give off steam even after each piece is thrown in. Their words are cited in Knesset Ha-gedola and Maḥazik Berakha 69:51. And in a time of need one may rely on them even ab initio, as will be explained below in halakha 9."}]},{"slug":"17-22-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-04/","title_he":"ד – הכנת הבשר למליחה","title_en":"Preparing the Meat for Salting","body_en":"<p>Before we detail the order of salting with its three stages – rinsing, salting and rinsing – it should be prefaced that salting is effective for drawing out the blood absorbed in the meat, but is not effective for blood that is collected and visible inside the large blood vessels, such as those in the shoulder, the jaw and the tongue, since this blood is considered as collected and standing in a vessel. Therefore, one must cut them before salting, so that the blood collected in them can come out (SA 65:1). Salting is also not effective for the blood vessels in the membranes of the brain and in the testicles of the male, which must be removed, or at least cut, so that the blood in them can come out through salting (Ḥullin 93a).</p><p>Meat in which blood has collected because of a blow, to the point that it has turned red, must be cut before salting, in order to make an opening for the blood so that it can come out through it. And if after the salting the meat still remains red, there is no prohibition in this. When meat is kashered by roasting, there is no need to cut the meat, because roasting has the power to draw out collected blood without a cut (SA 67:4; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 16).</p><p>The Geonim ruled that salting is effective on condition that three full days (72 hours) have not passed over the meat from the time of the sheḥita. But if they have passed, there is a concern that the blood in the meat has dried to the point that salting does not have the power to remove it. It can then be kashered only by roasting, because whatever the roasting does not remove will not come out afterwards either. But ab initio one should not cook this roast, lest there remain in the meat blood that the roasting did not succeed in removing, and it will come out in cooking. After the fact, if it was cooked after roasting, it is kosher. In order not to come to a stumbling block and kasher by salting meat that has remained for three full days, it is proper to be careful not to leave meat for three full days (SA and Rema 69:12). Liver may be left, since in any case its kashering is by roasting only (Ḥamudei Daniel, cited in Pitḥei Teshuva 26).</p><p>If before three full days have passed over the meat it is soaked in water, this halts the process of the blood drying, and it will be permitted to kasher it by salting for another three full days. After the fact, if the blood vessels are removed from it, one may make do with rinsing the meat in order to permit its salting for another three full days (SA 69:13; Taz 33).</p><p>Some forbid leaving meat for three full days even when part of the time it is frozen, out of concern that even while it is frozen the blood dries and sticks to the meat (Yaskil Avdi part 1, YD 3; Shevet Ha-levi 2:25). However, in practice, since during freezing the blood does not dry but freezes, in a time of need one may freeze meat for many months and afterwards salt it, since the duration of the freezing is not counted towards the three full days (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 69:79; Yad Yehuda 59; Yabi'a Omer 2, YD 4; 7, YD 4).</p><p>As long as the meat is frozen, salting and roasting are not effective for it, because they cannot draw out the blood in it. To thaw it, it is permitted to soak it in warm water that is not yad soledet bo (hot enough that the hand recoils from it), but it is forbidden to soak it in water that is yad soledet bo, lest it cook the meat and cause the blood to be absorbed in it so that it becomes forbidden (Beit Yosef 69:1).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-22-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-05/","title_he":"ה – הדחה","title_en":"Rinsing","body_en":"<p>There are three stages to kashering: rinsing, salting and rinsing, as the Sages said: meat is not freed of its blood unless one rinses it in water, salts it very well with salt, and again rinses it very well in water, in order to remove the salt with the blood in it (Ḥullin 113a).</p><p>There are several reasons for the rinsing before salting. First, in order to soften the meat, especially its surface, so as to allow the blood to flow out more easily (Ran and Haggahot Sha'arei Dura). Ab initio, so that the softening will be thorough, the custom is to soak the meat in water for half an hour. However, in a pressing situation, when there is no time for this, one may make do with a good rinse (Rema 69:1). In addition, if the meat is not rinsed of the blood on it, there is a concern that the salt will absorb the blood on the surface of the meat and will have no strength left to draw out the blood inside the meat (Ra'avya and Ra'avan).</p><p>A further reason: perhaps actual blood is stuck to the meat, and if it is not removed by rinsing, there is a concern that instead of the salt drawing the blood out of the meat, it will cause the blood on the surface of the meat to be absorbed into it with a power similar to cooking, since salt has the property that it sometimes cooks like something 'boiling' (below 35:8), and then the salting will no longer be able to remove the blood from the meat (Semag and Semak). However, in a pressing situation, meat that was salted without rinsing first is not forbidden; rather, one goes back, rinses it and salts it (SA and Rema 69:2).<sup>7</sup></p><p>Before salting, one should wait until the water drips off the meat, for otherwise the water will dissolve the salt and it will run off the meat, and the salt remaining on the surface of the meat will not have the power to draw the blood out from within the meat. But it is forbidden to wait until the meat dries, because then the salt will not stick to the meat and will not be able to draw out the blood (Rema 69:1; Shakh and Taz).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"The Pri Megadim, in his introduction to the laws of salting, arranged six opinions regarding the reason for the first rinsing, and some have divided the opinions differently. However, it appears that the Sages intended in their instruction all the reasons and benefits that there are in the rinsing before salting; but in certain cases the reason for the rinsing will have halakhic significance, and therefore the Rishonim defined certain reasons as the main ones in their opinion. For example, if the meat was cut after the first rinsing, according to the reason that the rinsing is meant to soften the meat, there is no need to rinse it again; and according to the reason that it is meant to remove the blood on the surface of the meat, one must rinse it again, and so it was ruled in practice (SA and Rema 69:1). Another example: if the meat was salted without rinsing first, according to the reason that the rinsing is meant to soften the meat or to enable the salt to draw the blood out of the meat, one can go back, rinse the meat and salt it again. But according to the reason that the salt is liable to cause dam be-ayin (actual blood) lying on the meat to be absorbed into it, there is no way to remedy the situation. In practice, SA 69:2 ruled without qualification in accordance with the lenient opinion and mentioned that some forbid, and the Rema wrote that in a case of great loss one may be lenient. The reasoning of the lenient opinion: either because they hold that the main reason for the rinsing is not to prevent the absorption of blood into the meat, or because after the fact they regard this blood too as dam pelita (exuded blood) that is not be-ayin, and consequently, even if the salt caused it to be absorbed in the meat, the subsequent salting will draw it out again (Binat Adam 27).<br>It is preferable that the water in which the meat is rinsed not be very cold, as the Beit Yosef 69:1 wrote in explaining the Ra'avya, that if the water is very cold there is a concern that it will seal in the blood and prevent it from coming out. And so wrote several Aḥaronim (Knesset Ha-gedola, Dagul Me-revava, Pri Megadim, Pitḥei Teshuva 69:1; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 6). Based on this, some ruled for mehadrin kashrut that the water should be at a temperature of 10 degrees, and at least 5-7 degrees (Naharot Eitan part 2, YD 23). The instructions of the Chief Rabbinate are also that it be at least 8 degrees. On the other hand, according to most poskim, as long as the water flows, that is, it is not frozen, one may rinse in it. Firstly, this is so according to the reason that the rinsing is meant to remove the blood from the surface of the meat. And even according to the reason that it is meant to soften the meat, according to several Aḥaronim the water may be very cold (Tuv Ta'am Va-da'at Telita'i 133; Tiferet Tzvi YD 3; Ḥazon Ish YD 10:4). And since according to veterinary supervision the water should be cooled as much as possible in order to prevent the development of diseases, one should prefer the health-related enhancement of cooling the water over the enhancement of using lukewarm water, which is correct only according to some of the poskim, in one of the explanations of the rinsing."}]},{"slug":"17-22-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-06/","title_he":"ו – המליחה","title_en":"Salting","body_en":"<p>Salt is spread over the meat until no noticeable place on it remains without salt, and the meat is salted until it reaches a state in which, because of how salty it is, it would not be fit to eat; there is no need to add more than that.</p><p>The salt should be medium-grained, for if it is as fine as flour, there is a concern that it will be absorbed in the meat and lose its drawing power, and if it is very coarse, there is a concern that it will fall off the meat (SA 69:3). The recommended salt is called 'coarse salt for cooking'. After the fact, one may use even salt as fine as flour (Rema), and all the more so our regular salt, which is not as fine as flour.</p><p>The meat is salted on all its sides; after the fact, if the meat was salted on one side only and cooked, the meat is permitted. Salting all sides of the meat is not effective for the internal organs, each of which requires salting in its own right. Therefore, with a bird, the internal organs are taken out, and the bird is salted outside and inside, and the internal organs are salted separately on all their sides.<sup>8</sup></p><p>The salted meat must be placed on a perforated vessel or on a tray placed at an incline, so that the blood that comes out of the meat will drip down or flow down the slope; for if it remains adjacent to the meat, there is a concern that it will be absorbed in it again, and there is also a concern that it will block the way for the blood still remaining in the meat to come out (SA 69:16-18).<sup>9</sup></p><p>Ab initio, the meat should be left in the salt for an hour, and in a time of need one may make do with leaving it for eighteen minutes. And if the meat remained in its salt for less than eighteen minutes and was cooked, since blood still remained in it, the meat is forbidden to be eaten (SA 69:6; Rema 11; Taz 30).</p><p>Whether the piece of meat is as small as a kezayit or half a metre thick, the meat is left in its salt for the same length of time, for the Sages did not differentiate in their measures. For the salt draws out all the blood that can flow freely from it, and the liquids that are not drawn out of it, even though their colour is red, are not considered blood from the standpoint of halakha.<sup>10</sup></p><p>One may salt many pieces of meat and place them in a pile one on top of the other until the blood comes out of them. And even if among them there are pieces of beef that exude a lot of blood and pieces of chicken that exude a little blood, there is no concern that the blood that came out of the beef will enter the chicken, because meat exudes its blood, or the brine that comes out of it, for twelve hours from the salting, and throughout that time it does not absorb blood that flows over it. And even if there is in the pile a piece that has a receptacle (a hollow) in which blood that flowed from other pieces collects, it does not become forbidden, and after the salting it is rinsed like the other pieces and is thereby kashered. However, ab initio a piece of meat that has a hollow should be placed facing downwards, so that blood that comes out during salting will not collect in it (SA and Rema 70:1, 5-6).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"According to the Rashba, if the meat was salted on one side, or a bird on its outer or inner side, after the fact the salting was effective in drawing out the blood from all the meat. And so wrote the Orḥot Ḥayim, the Maggid Mishneh, the Ran and SA 69:4. On the other hand, according to the Roke'aḥ, the Mordekhai, the Issur Ve-heter He-arokh and the Yam Shel Shlomo, salting is effective in drawing out the blood near the salted side, and therefore one must salt both sides, and if it was salted on one side – it is forbidden. And the Rema 69:4 explained that if twelve hours have not yet passed, one may salt it on the other or inner side and thereby kasher it for cooking. And if twelve hours have passed, it can be kashered only by roasting. The Rema further wrote that if one cooked after salting on one side, in a time of need one may rely on the lenient opinion. Above I leaned slightly towards the SA's side and wrote that after the fact, if one cooked it, it is permitted, since blood that was cooked is forbidden rabbinically, and a doubt regarding a rabbinic law is treated leniently. On the other hand, even though the SA was lenient after the fact to cook meat that had been salted on one side without remedying it, since it is possible to remedy it, I wrote that one should be stringent and remedy it as the Rema explained, and as the Erekh Ha-shulḥan 69:8, the Zivḥei Tzedek 58 and the Ben Ish Ḥai (Year Two, Aḥarei Mot 2) wrote. Even though one must salt the internal organs on all their sides (Pri Megadim 69, Mishbetzot Zahav 15), the custom is not to take out the kidneys that are sunk in the thighs of the bird, because they are very small and perhaps also more firmly attached to the bird, and one relies on their salting from one side."},{"n":9,"en":"Above, in note 3, the dispute was cited as to whether blood that moved from place to place within the meat is forbidden, and the law of one who salts meat in a vessel that is not perforated depends on this dispute. According to many, only the part of the meat that was immersed in the blood, plus the measure of a peel (kelipa), becomes forbidden, since the blood is absorbed in it again with great force, and it is then impossible to remove it even by roasting (SA 69:18). And according to Rabbeinu Peretz and the Rema, all of the meat becomes forbidden, since the blood inside the meat has already moved to come out of its place, and in their view its main exit is downwards; and since it cannot come out downwards, because it is blocked by the blood that collected there, it has begun to separate within the meat and has not come out, and consequently all the meat becomes forbidden. And according to the Rashba, the blood also comes out from the sides; therefore, even though in his opinion blood that moved from its place within the meat is forbidden, here, where it can come out from the sides, the halakha follows the lenient opinion and only the part immersed in the blood is forbidden."},{"n":10,"en":"The Geonim wrote that the measure of the duration of salting is like the measure of roasting, since both draw out the blood, and even though roasting draws with greater force, the duration of drawing is equal. The Rishonim disputed the explanation of their words: according to the Or Zarua and the Sha'arei Dura, the intent is according to the time needed to roast that particular piece. That is, if the piece is large, since the time to roast it is long, the time to salt it is also as long. And according to many, one cannot say this, for it is not possible that the law was given to varying measures; not only that, but even when the size of the piece is known, there is no fixed measure for the time of its roasting, since it depends on the strength of the fire. Therefore they wrote that the measure of roasting is the time it takes to walk a mil (Rambam; Rashi; Ra'avya; SA 69:6). And indeed, on a very strong fire, within this measure one can half-roast even a very large piece. The Terumat Ha-deshen (167) wrote that many are accustomed ab initio to wait about an hour, which suffices to roast the largest piece completely; and so wrote the Rema 69:6 and the Ben Ish Ḥai (Year Two, Teharot 17), that it is proper to act this way ab initio, and in a time of need one may make do with the measure of a mil. The measure of a mil according to the SA is 18 minutes (and so too in SA OḤ 459:2). And even though some are stringent to regard it as 22.5 minutes, or 24 minutes (see BHL 459:2), according to most poskim it is 18 minutes – all the more so in this law, since blood that was cooked is forbidden rabbinically."}]},{"slug":"17-22-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-07/","title_he":"ז – ההדחה שלאחר המליחה","title_en":"The Rinsing after Salting","body_en":"<p>After the salting is completed, the meat must be rinsed in water; in practice the poskim ruled that the meat should be rinsed in water three times, each time in fresh water that has no remnants of blood in it. When the meat is rinsed under a stream of water from the tap, there is no need to do it three times; rather, it should be rinsed until not a trace of blood or salt remains on it.<sup>11</sup></p><p>The salt with which the meat was salted, even if it has dried and it is not evident that blood is absorbed in it, is forbidden to be eaten, and one should not salt further meat with it (Rema 69:9).</p><p>Meat that was properly salted, and afterwards a red liquid flows out of it – from the standpoint of halakha this is not considered blood but 'ḥemar basar' (meat juice), that is, a red fluid that comes out of the meat. And even if the meat was cut and a red liquid flowed from the place of the cut, according to the overwhelming majority of poskim there is no prohibition in this liquid, since after salting all the red liquid remaining in the meat is fit to be eaten (as explained in note 5).</p><p>However, according to the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 6:10), salting is effective for removing the blood from the outer part of the meat only, but blood still remains in the inner part; therefore, after the second rinsing, the salted meat must be scalded by putting it into boiling water, so that all the blood that remains in it will be sealed in and stick to the meat and will no longer come out in cooking, and then it is dam evarim that has not separated, which involves no prohibition. And if the meat was not scalded in boiling water, the red liquid that comes out of it by itself or as a result of cutting the meat is forbidden as blood. And if the meat is cooked, the meat will become forbidden. But if after scalding a red liquid comes out of the meat, this is the fluid (mohal), which involves no prohibition.</p><p>In practice, the prevalent custom is not to be concerned for the opinion of the Rambam (Rema; Baḥ; Levush; Pri Ḥadash). Only among immigrants from Yemen are there some who are accustomed to be stringent according to his words. And some say that the Rambam's words were said regarding salting of eighteen minutes, but if the meat is left in the salt for an hour, as is customary today, all the blood comes out or is sealed into the meat, and even according to the Rambam there is no need afterwards to scald the meat in boiling water. And even if a red liquid flows out of it, there is no prohibition in it (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 69:36-40); and even those of Yemenite origin who are accustomed to be stringent and scald the meat may rely on this in a time of need.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"See SA and Rema 69:7 and the commentators, that there are different opinions in explaining the number of rinsings, and I wrote according to the opinion accepted by all. However, a good rinse in running water removes the blood and the salt better, and therefore it is valid ab initio; and so wrote Rav Yabrov in Divrei Sofrim 76, Emek Davar 175. If the meat was rinsed once and afterwards cooked, the meat is kosher, since by the letter of the law one rinsing suffices to remove the salt and the blood on the meat, and even if a trace of salt or blood remained on the meat, it is certainly nullified in sixty. And if it was cooked without being rinsed even once, if the dish contains sixty times the salt and blood on it – the dish is permitted, and if not – the dish is forbidden (Beit Yosef; SA and Rema 69:9)."},{"n":12,"en":"Above in halakha 3 and note 5, the opinion of the overwhelming majority of poskim was presented at length, among them: the Ra'avad, the Rashba, the Ran, the Rivash, the Maggid Mishneh, the Baḥ, the Levush and many others. And in accordance with the Rambam wrote the Ra'ah and the Ritva. Seemingly, according to the Rambam there is no need for salting; but the Ran explained that if one does not salt, there is a concern that the scalding will cause the outer side of the meat to exude blood and will then cause it to be absorbed in the meat again, and it will become forbidden; therefore one must salt it first in order to remove the blood near the outside, and afterwards the scalding will seal in what remains in it. The Beit Yosef wrote that where possible it is good to be concerned for the opinion of the Rambam, and so the Shakh 69:78 explained the opinion of SA 69:19. And the Arukh Ha-shulḥan 69:36-40 explained that even the Rambam would agree that if the salting lasted an hour, there is no further need for scalding. And so wrote the Netziv (Ha'amek She'ela, Vayikra 68:13). Rav Kafiḥ, in his commentary on the Rambam, did not agree with this, and in the Shulḥan Arukh Ha-mekutzar 132:13-14 he agreed with it in a time of need, such as when staying as a guest in other places. And in my humble opinion, immigrants from Yemen may rely on the Arukh Ha-shulḥan's explanation of the Rambam's opinion even when they are at home, since it is a doubt regarding a rabbinic law (see above, note 4), and the custom has weakened, for they are accustomed to be lenient when studying in yeshivot and when staying as guests, and there are even Torah scholars among the immigrants from Yemen who are accustomed to be lenient in this at home. However, there is an important enhancement in this, and members of all the communities who wish to be mehadrin, and especially immigrants from Yemen, would do well to be mehadrin in accordance with the Rambam."}]},{"slug":"17-22-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-08/","title_he":"ח – איברים מיוחדים","title_en":"Special Organs","body_en":"<p><b>Bones</b> that contain marrow require 'rinsing, salting and rinsing' like meat, and the salting done on the bone is effective for the marrow inside. And if the meat was salted with the bones, the salting of the meat is effective for the bones as well (Rema 71:3; Shakh 11).</p><p><b>Heart</b>: Blood is collected inside the chambers of the heart; therefore, one must first cut the heart and rinse out the blood remaining inside its chambers, and afterwards salt or roast it. After the fact, if the heart was salted or roasted without cleaning it, it should be cut and the blood collected in its chambers rinsed out (Mishna Ḥullin 109a; SA 72:1-2).</p><p><b>The intestines</b>, from the stomach to the rectum, do not require salting, since they are not presumed to be organs that contain blood. That is, their blood is so slight that it is not noticeable, and there is no concern that it will leave its place. Only if there is a place in the intestines that has a red appearance – this indicates that blood has collected there and it must be salted. The fat on the intestines, like other fat and meat, requires salting. As for the rumen (keres), the reticulum (beit ha-kosot) and the omasum (hemses), even though some are lenient to regard them as included among the intestines that do not require salting (SA 75:1), in practice they must be salted like all meat (Rema; Pri Ḥadash; Maḥazik Berakha; Zivḥei Tzedek).</p><p><b>Liver and spleen</b>: The liver is all blood, and therefore it is kashered only by roasting (below 12). The spleen, however, even though its colour is red like the liver, has the law of other meat, for which salting is effective. The spleen of a domestic animal must be peeled of the ḥelev attached to it (SA and Rema 74:1). And since peeling it involves great effort, the custom is not to kasher it but to sell it to non-Jews.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-22-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-09/","title_he":"ט – שאלת המלח לחולים","title_en":"The Question of Salt for the Ill","body_en":"<p>There are sick people for whom salt is harmful to their health, such as those suffering from high blood pressure, and for them eating meat that was properly salted is not healthy, because even though the salt is rinsed off the meat, part of it is absorbed in the meat and is dangerous for them. The first solution is to kasher the meat by roasting, since in this way there is no need to use salt. And even though ab initio the meat is salted a little before roasting, in a time of need one may be lenient about this (below, halakha 10). When the sick person cannot eat roasted meat, one may cook the meat after roasting in order to soften it.</p><p>Likewise, one may kasher a very large piece of meat by salting, and afterwards cut off the outer layer of the meat, in which the saltiness is perceptible, and give the sick person from the inner part, which has no saltiness.</p><p>When these solutions are not suitable, one may kasher the meat by scalding (above 3). True, the Geonim were stringent ab initio not to kasher by scalding; however, in a pressing situation they ruled to kasher the meat by scalding small pieces, taking care that before each piece is thrown into the water, the water boils and gives off steam (Knesset Ha-gedola and Maḥazik Berakha 69:51). And in this manner it is permitted to scald for the needs of a sick person (Yeshu'at Moshe 47; Shemirat Shabbat Ke-hilkhata 40:100; above, note 6).<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"Some have proposed a further solution: to use a special salt intended for these patients, that is, instead of sodium-based salt, which is the one that is dangerous to health, a salt based on potassium or ammonium, which in various tests was found to be as effective in removing blood as regular salt (Beit Avi 1:89; Shemirat Shabbat Ke-hilkhata 40:100). On the other hand, some do not rely on the tests, and in their opinion Ḥazal said to salt only with regular salt that contains sodium; and as long as it has not been proven with certainty that these substitutes are exactly as effective as regular salt, meat should not be kashered with them (Mishpetei Uziel part 2, YD 7; She'ilat Yeshurun 1, YD 13). In a pressing situation, when there is no other solution, one may rely on the lenient opinion, since the dispute concerns a rabbinic law, as blood that was cooked is forbidden rabbinically."}]},{"slug":"17-22-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-10/","title_he":"י – צלייה","title_en":"Roasting","body_en":"<p>Just as salting kashers the meat by removing the blood, so roasting kashers the meat by removing the blood and burning it. And the power of roasting is greater than that of salting: salting is not effective for meat that has remained three days, because the blood in it has already dried, whereas roasting is effective for it (above 4). Likewise, salting cannot remove blood that has collected in the meat without cutting the place where it collected, whereas roasting removes it even without cutting the place where the blood collected (above 4). Salting is also not effective for the liver, since it is all blood, but roasting is effective for it (below 12).</p><p>This is the order of roasting: the meat is rinsed of the remnants of blood on it. Close to the roasting, it is desirable to salt it a little. It is roasted until it is fit to be eaten, and thereby it becomes kosher. In greater detail:</p><p>Before kashering the meat by roasting, it is rinsed of the blood on it, because some say there is a concern that perhaps the roasting will cause the blood on the meat to be absorbed into it. After the fact, if the meat was roasted without rinsing, as long as it is blood that came out of it, it is kosher. And if the blood on it came from other meat, the roast becomes forbidden (to the depth of a netila, or all of it, as cited in the note).</p><p>Ab initio, close to the roasting, a little salt is put on the meat, so that the salt will help the fire draw out the blood. And if salt was not put on, the meat is kosher. After salting the meat, it must be put on the fire immediately, for if the meat remains in its salt for several minutes, blood will begin to come out of it, and then it will be necessary to rinse the blood off the meat again before roasting. However, after the fact, if one forgot to rinse it, the roasting is valid, because roasting has the power to kasher the meat even while the blood that flowed from it is lying on it (SA and Rema 76:2).<sup>14</sup></p><p>Some are accustomed to rinse the meat after roasting, to ensure that not a trace of blood remains on it (Rema 73:5; 76:2). However, when one wishes to eat the meat roasted and rinsing would spoil its taste, it is permitted to eat it without rinsing, for there is a presumption that the fire has burnt all the blood that came out of it.<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"If there was dam be-ayin on the meat, that is, blood that came from other meat, it must be rinsed before roasting. And if it was not rinsed, the fire does not have the power to draw it out, and the meat becomes forbidden to the depth of a netila (a thickness of 1.9 cm) in the place where the blood was on it (Tosafot Ḥullin 113a, s.v. 'ein'; SA 76:1). And some say that if the meat does not contain sixty times its volume, all of it becomes forbidden (Taz 1; Shakh 5). But if the blood on it is the blood that came out of it, according to most Rishonim there is no need to rinse it before roasting (Tosafot, Rashba, Rosh and Ran), and so it was ruled in SA 76:1-2. And the Rema 76:2 wrote that ab initio one should rinse it and salt it, because it is proper to take into account two opinions of Rishonim: 1) The opinion of the Ramban and the Ran, that one must rinse the meat of the blood on it, for otherwise the fire may cause the blood to be absorbed in the meat. 2) The opinion of Rashi, the Rambam and the Ra'avad, that the roast requires a little salting so that it will exude its blood. However, after the fact, if one neither rinsed nor salted, the Rema too agrees that the roasting kashered the meat, in accordance with the opinion of most Rishonim, and it is not clear that even the stringent opinions were stringent after the fact. And even though according to the SA there is no need to rinse and salt a little, I wrote that it is proper to act this way so as not to multiply disputes (le-afushei pelugta) between Sephardim and Ashkenazim, especially since even among the distinguished Sephardic poskim there are some who ruled to act this way ab initio, as the Knesset Ha-gedola and the Ḥida in Maḥazik Berakha 76:3 wrote. And the Zivḥei Tzedek 76:8 wrote that this was the custom in Baghdad."},{"n":15,"en":"The great majority of the Rishonim did not mention rinsing after roasting, among them: the Rashba, the Ran and SA 76:2. However, according to the Sha'arei Dura, if one did not salt the meat before roasting, the custom is to rinse it after roasting. And according to the Issur Ve-heter He-arokh, if one intends to cook the roast, the custom is to rinse it. And according to the Rema 73:5; 76:2, the custom is to rinse it ab initio even when eating it roasted, and after the fact, if it was not rinsed, it is kosher. And according to the Rema's custom of rinsing it, some say that it must be rinsed three times (Shakh 76:15), and some say once (Rashal). And even though it is customary to write that Ashkenazim are accustomed to be stringent in this ab initio, when it involves difficulty one should rule for everyone according to the essential halakha, in accordance with the opinion of the Sephardic poskim (Beit Yosef; Pri To'ar 76:16; Shulḥan Gavoha 19; Zivḥei Tzedek 33). And even among the Ashkenazi poskim there are some who were lenient in this ab initio (Levush 73:2; Gra 8), and even the stringent ones were stringent in this only ab initio. Therefore, when rinsing spoils the taste of the roast, one may rely on the great majority of the Rishonim, who did not write to rinse after roasting. Moreover, it is possible that even according to the stringent opinions, the main view is that of the Haggahot Sha'arei Dura, that if the meat was roasted completely, as it is customarily roasted in order to prepare it for eating, there is no need to rinse it. Indeed, in Ashkenaz the stringent found a solution for this: they would roast a little, rinse, and roast again, so that the taste of roasting would remain (Rashal). However, since this involves effort and distress, and even the stringent opinions agree that the meat is kosher without rinsing, and we do not multiply disputes, those of Ashkenazi origin too may refrain from rinsing after roasting. And perhaps the whole reason they were stringent is that in Ashkenaz they were generally accustomed to cook the roast, and it was not difficult to be mehadrin in this."}]},{"slug":"17-22-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-11/","title_he":"יא – שיעור הצלייה ודרכיה","title_en":"The Measure of Roasting and Its Methods","body_en":"<p>The roasting that kashers the meat is roasting the meat until it would be fit, in a pressing situation, to be eaten by most people, which is the measure of half its roasting, that is, about half the time required for complete roasting, in which the roast becomes excellent and fit for all people. After that, even if a red fluid comes out of the meat, from the standpoint of halakha it is not considered blood but \"ḥemar basar\", that is, meat juice (\"meat wine\"), and it is permitted to eat it (Rema 76:2).</p><p>When one wishes to cook the roast, it is good to roast it completely.<sup>16</sup></p><p>It is permitted and even desirable to turn the roast from side to side so that all of it will be roasted well. And one may turn it again several times. However, ab initio it is good to avoid rolling it continuously and quickly, because some are concerned that in this way the blood that came out of the meat will roll over it and be absorbed in it again (Rema 76:2; Minḥat Yaakov 9:6). But after the fact, if one did so, the meat is kosher, because even in this way the fire burns the blood that comes out of the meat (Yad Yehuda, Arokh 7).</p><p>It is permitted to roast many pieces of meat at once, and there is no concern that the blood that flows from the upper piece will be absorbed in the piece below it, since the property of blood that is drawn out of meat by roasting or salting is that it is 'mesharek sharik', that is, it slides off and is not absorbed.</p><p>When a large pile of pieces of meat is roasted at once, every piece that has become fit, in a pressing situation, to be eaten by most people is kosher. And one may take it out of the pile and eat it, even though there are still pieces above it and beside it that have not yet been kashered.<sup>17</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"As we learned in note 3, according to the Rashba and the Rema, blood that moved from place to place within the meat, even though it did not come out, is forbidden. And according to the Rosh, the Ra'ah and the SA, as long as it did not come out, it is permitted. According to the Rashba and the Rema, the meat must be roasted in such a way that all its blood comes out, for otherwise it will move from its place without coming out and the meat will become forbidden; therefore it must be roasted half-way (ḥatzi tzeliya), at which point all the forbidden blood comes out (Rema 76:2; Shakh 13). And some say that even according to the Rashba, if one roasted less than half-way, it is kosher, because the fire has the power to quickly draw out all the blood that was dislodged from its place (Arukh Ha-shulḥan; Yabi'a Omer 8, YD 9). And according to the Rosh and the SA, as long as one does not intend to cook the roast afterwards, even with less than half-roasting the meat is kosher to eat. And even though this is the halakha according to most poskim, and one who wishes to rely on them may do so, I did not mention their opinion above, since it is customary to roast at least half-way, and consequently why should one not fulfil the obligation according to all the poskim? (When roasting a large piece of meat, it is worthwhile to roast the meat over a low fire for a long time, so that the inner part of the meat too will reach half-roasting and be fit to eat.)<br>If one intends to cook the roast afterwards, some say that it must be roasted half-way, at which point all its blood comes out (Eshkol; Rosh in the name of the Geonim; Issur Ve-heter He-arokh; Rema 76:2), and some say that this is the opinion of all the Rishonim (Baḥ; Shakh 76:14; Pri Ḥadash). And some say that according to the Or Zarua and Rabbeinu Peretz, the fluid that remains after half-roasting is forbidden as blood, and if one cooks what was half-roasted, the blood will come out in the cooking; therefore a roast that is going to be cooked must be roasted completely. Many Aḥaronim took their opinion into account (Yam Shel Shlomo, Knesset Ha-gedola, Beit Leḥem Yehuda, Maḥazik Berakha, Ben Ish Ḥai. And some even wrote that since it is difficult to discern when complete roasting has been reached, it is good to roast the meat on both its sides until it dries on the outside; see Taz 69:54). In practice, when the custom of the stringent involves no difficulty or loss, since in any case the roast will be cooked afterwards, I wrote above that it is good to act in accordance with them and roast completely. And if one cooked after half-roasting – it is kosher."},{"n":17,"en":"According to the Rema 77:1, ab initio one should not roast a piece of meat that requires kashering together with a piece of meat that has already been kashered, or with another food such as potatoes, lest the kashered piece, which does not exude brine, or the other food absorb some of the blood of the piece that has not yet been kashered, and lest the roasting not have the power to draw out this blood that came to them from outside. However, after the fact one should not forbid it, because \"as it absorbs, so it expels\". And according to the SA, it is permitted to roast them together even ab initio."}]},{"slug":"17-22-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-12/","title_he":"יב – דין הכבד","title_en":"The Law of the Liver","body_en":"<p>The liver is full of blood, to the point that it was said of it that it is 'all blood'; nevertheless it is permitted to eat it, for the Torah forbade only blood that is poured out like water, and the liver is considered like congealed blood (Levush).</p><p>According to Rabbeinu Tam, a liver can be kashered by salting, except that since it is full of blood, before the salting one must cut it lengthwise and widthwise (crosswise), and place it with the cuts facing downwards, so that the blood in it will flow out of it. However, in practice it was ruled that a liver should not be kashered by salting, because the liver is full of blood, and salting does not have the power to remove from it all the blood required according to halakha. Therefore, only by roasting can a liver be kashered for eating, and after it has been properly roasted, it is permitted to cook it as well.</p><p>The large vessels in the liver should be cut before roasting it, so that the blood can flow out of them easily. In practice, since the gall bladder attached to the liver is cut off, the required opening is created, and therefore there is no obligation to cut further into the liver before roasting (and see the note).<sup>18</sup></p><p>Ab initio, when roasting liver together with a piece of regular meat, one should not place the liver on the regular meat, since the liver has a lot of blood that will flow onto it. After the fact, the meat under the liver is kosher, since the abundant blood that comes out of the liver is 'mesharek sharik' (slides off) and is not absorbed in the piece beneath it, which is itself in the process of being kashered by the roasting (Ḥullin 111a; SA 73:4).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":18,"en":"Blood that comes out of the liver, according to the Rambam, Ramban, Rashba and Ran, is forbidden by Torah law. And according to Rabbeinu Tam, the Issur Ve-heter He-arokh, the Rashal and the Shakh, it is forbidden rabbinically, and only the blood collected in the simponot (vessels) of the liver is forbidden by Torah law (Siftei Da'at 73:14). According to Rabbeinu Tam, the Ramban and the Rashba, a liver can be kashered by salting after it has been torn crosswise. Whereas according to Rashi, the Ritva and the Ran, salting is not effective for kashering a liver, and all the cutting is meant for kashering by roasting. In practice, the Geonim wrote that a liver can be kashered only by roasting, and the Rishonim disputed whether it must be cut beforehand. According to the Rif, the Rambam and others, since it is kashered by roasting, there is no need to cut it beforehand. According to the Behag, Rashi and others, it must be cut before roasting, and so wrote SA 73:3. According to Tosafot and the Rosh, if one intends to eat it roasted, there is no need to cut it, and if one intends to cook it after roasting, it must be cut before kashering it by roasting; and so wrote the Rema.<br>In practice, since the gall bladder is removed with a little of the liver before roasting, the simponot are thereby cut, and this is effective both for a chicken liver and for the liver of a domestic animal (Rema and most poskim; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 10). And some say that for the liver of a domestic animal, which is large, removing the gall bladder is not sufficient (Taz 2; Ḥavat Da'at); but since in any case it is customary to cut the liver of a domestic animal into slices before roasting it, there is no need to cut it crosswise, and therefore I did not detail this law above. (And some are stringent to cut even a chicken liver crosswise; Darkhei Teshuva 73:24.)<br>Concerning a liver that was not kashered and was cooked, it is said in Mishna Terumot 10:11: \"The liver renders forbidden but does not become forbidden, because it exudes and does not absorb.\" Thus, by the letter of the law one may cook a liver without kashering and eat it, but whatever was cooked with it is forbidden. However, the Tanna'im (Ḥullin 111a) and the poskim disputed the law of a liver that was shaluk (cooked for a prolonged time). According to Rabbi Yishmael son of Rabbi Yoḥanan ben Beroka, it is forbidden, since after the end of ordinary cooking it stops exuding and goes back to absorbing the blood that came out of it; and Rabbeinu Peretz, the Issur Ve-heter He-arokh and the Rema 73:1 took this opinion into account. And according to Rabbi Eliezer, even a shaluk liver is permitted, because even during the continued prolonged cooking it only exudes and does not absorb (and this is also the opinion of Rabbi Yoḥanan ben Nuri in Mishna Terumot 10:11). And so ruled the Ramban, the Rashba and the Rosh. And the Geonim ruled not to cook liver, and they disputed the reason for the prohibition: some say it is because they were concerned for the opinion that a shaluk liver is forbidden and people would not know how to distinguish between cooked and shaluk (Rabbeinu Peretz, Issur Ve-heter He-arokh); and some say that even though a shaluk liver is permitted, they were concerned that people would cook the liver with other foods and forget that they become forbidden, and likewise regarding the pot, they would forget that it became forbidden (Ritva, and so it appears from the Ramban, Rashba and Rosh). And the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 6:8) has a different view, that even a liver that was cooked is forbidden. In practice, after the fact, if they transgressed the instruction of the Geonim and cooked liver, according to SA 73:1, in its unattributed ruling, it is permitted (and the implication is that this is the law for shaluk as well), and the Rema wrote that the custom is to forbid it, and so wrote the Shakh 8."}]},{"slug":"17-22-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-13/","title_he":"יג – האש וכלי הצלייה","title_en":"The Fire and the Roasting Utensils","body_en":"<p>Roasting can be done over an open fire, when the meat is held on a grill or on a skewer and the blood that comes out of it flows into the fire. One may also roast meat by placing it directly on a plate heated by a fire burning beneath it or by electric heating elements beneath it. If the plate is smooth, the blood that comes out of the meat flows to the sides, and the little that remains under the meat is burnt up. But if it is rough or has depressions, it is proper to place it at an incline, so that the blood that comes out of the meat will flow downwards and not be trapped under the meat. It is forbidden to roast meat on a vessel or surface that has a receptacle, since the blood that comes out in roasting cannot flow to the sides and consequently will be absorbed in the meat and render it forbidden permanently, just as cooking meat that was not salted renders it forbidden permanently.<sup>19</sup></p><p>It is permitted to roast meat inside a gas or electric oven, and it is permitted to close the oven during roasting. And it makes no difference whether the heating elements are under the meat, above it or beside it: as long as the meat is roasted properly and the blood has a place to flow, the meat is kashered. So that the bottom of the oven will not become dirty and even forbidden because of absorbing the taste of the blood, a vessel should be placed at the bottom of the oven to catch the blood that flows from the meat.<sup>20</sup></p><p>Some are stringent not to cut with a knife meat that is on the fire before its kashering is complete, because the knife will absorb some of the blood and it will be necessary to kasher it by libun (heating to glowing) on the fire. And some are stringent and forbid the skewer on which the meat is roasted, because it absorbs some of the blood; therefore, before roasting further meat on it, it must be kashered by libun. And some are stringent and instruct to remove the meat from the skewer immediately after taking it off the fire, for otherwise there is a concern that the meat will absorb the taste of blood from the skewer.</p><p>However, in practice it was ruled in accordance with the permissive opinion in these three laws, since the act of roasting is a kashering, and if it kashers the forbidden meat, all the more so it does not render forbidden the utensils that touch the meat during the process of its kashering (Rosh; SA 76:4). True, some say that ab initio one should take into account the stringent opinion, and this is the custom of many of Ashkenazi origin. However, in practice, those of Ashkenazi origin too may be lenient in accordance with the majority of Rishonim, and use a skewer, a grill and a knife without libun.<sup>21</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":19,"en":"The Tzlaḥ (Pesaḥim 74a) raised a doubt whether perhaps only an open fire is effective for removing the blood; however, the halakha was ruled that heat too is effective like an open fire (Yad Efrayim; Ḥikrei Lev; Ben Ish Ḥai). After it was ruled that any heat is effective, some say that if the meat is placed on a surface, it must have holes so that the blood will not collect and will be able to flow through them (Tzlaḥ; Ḥavat Da'at 68:1). And according to most poskim, as long as the surface has no receptacle in which the blood can collect, even if it is rough like earthenware and has depressions like the floor of ovens, it is permitted to roast meat on it (Yad Efrayim; Ḥikrei Lev; Ben Ish Ḥai; Halikhot Olam part 6, Kedoshim 2; see Tzitz Eliezer 11:53)."},{"n":20,"en":"Some hold that if one roasts inside an oven, ab initio one must leave its door open, for otherwise the vapours and smell that come out of the blood may render the roast and the oven forbidden; however, after the fact, even according to their view, if one roasted in a closed oven, the liver [?] is permitted (Be-ohala Shel Torah 1:11). However, many did not mention this stringency, because they were not concerned about the sweat (vapour) and smell coming out of the blood, since they are slight and dry up immediately in the heat of the oven (see below 25:14; 35:10; Ḥeshev Ha-efod 2:114).<br>If no vessel was placed to catch the blood and it flowed onto the bottom of the oven, the oven as a whole is kosher, since there is no concern that the taste of the blood on its bottom will reach what is baked in it in a baking pan; but it is forbidden to place food on the bottom of the oven that absorbed blood. Its kashering is by heating the oven again at the same temperature. And if there is a concern that the heating will not burn the remnants of blood on the bottom, they should be cleaned off before heating."},{"n":21,"en":"According to the Semag, it is forbidden to cut with a knife meat that is on the fire whose kashering has not yet been completed, because the knife will absorb the taste of blood and become forbidden. But the skewer on which meat is roasted, since it remains on the fire until the meat on it is kashered, also becomes kosher, for \"as it absorbs, so it expels\" (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 76:17). And according to the Rashba, the skewer on which meat was kashered by roasting becomes forbidden, and if one wishes to use it again, one must kasher it by libun. The reason for the prohibition in his opinion: according to the Pri Megadim, because it absorbs and does not expel as easily as the meat; and according to the Pri Ḥadash, because the meat is pressed tightly against the skewer and does not allow it to expel there. And the Ra'avad added that it is forbidden to leave the meat on the skewer after it has been removed from the fire, lest it absorb some of the taste of the blood absorbed in the skewer. On the other hand, according to the Rosh, the Mordekhai, the Haggahot Asheri and the Tur, all this is permitted, since if the action of the roasting fire permits the meat by drawing out the blood and burning it, all the more so it does not render the utensils forbidden. And the SA 76:4 wrote that the custom is to be lenient, and so wrote the later Sephardic authorities (see Halikhot Olam part 6, Kedoshim 4). However, the Rema wrote, based on the Mahari (Isserlein), that ab initio one should be stringent to kasher the knife and the skewer by libun, and after the fact, if they were not kashered by libun, the meat is kosher (see Magen Avraham 509:12). And some say that the roasting grill too requires kashering (see Darkhei Teshuva 76:38). However, in truth, even according to the Rema one may be lenient with a grill, because its grip on the meat is not as strong as that of a skewer, and so it was testified in the name of the Ḥazon Ish.<br>In the books of our time it is written that those of Ashkenazi origin must be stringent in accordance with the Rema. However, the prevalent custom in Ashkenaz was to be lenient, as mentioned in the Yad Yehuda (76, Katzar 26). And it is possible that since this is a remote concern, and in practice one never perceives the taste of blood in the skewer, and in addition it is a doubt regarding a rabbinic law, since blood that was cooked is forbidden rabbinically, they followed the lenient opinion. It may further be said that the whole stringency applies when roasting half-way, but when roasting completely, as is customary, even the stringent opinions may agree that in the process the skewer is kashered as well (and so wrote the Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh of Pfeuffer 5:4:9; and see 'Divrei Sofrim', Emek Davar 76, 253; 257-258)."}]},{"slug":"17-22-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-22-14/","title_he":"יד – דם דגים, חגבים ואדם","title_en":"The Blood of Fish, Locusts and Humans","body_en":"<p>The prohibition of blood applies only to species that, when they are pure, must be slaughtered in order for their meat to be permitted to be eaten, that is, species of domestic animal, wild animal and bird, and not fish and locusts (above, halakha 2). However, because of mar'it ayin (appearances), when it is not evident that it is the blood of fish or locusts, it is forbidden by the words of the Sages to eat it, lest people learn from this to eat forbidden blood. Therefore, when the blood of fish is collected in a vessel, it is forbidden by the words of the Sages to drink it, and only if it is evident that it is fish blood, such as when some scales or parts of fish were put in with it, is it permitted (SA 66:9). And if the blood of fish or of locusts became mixed into food, as long as it is not discernible – the mixture is permitted (Rema 66:10).</p><p>There is no Torah prohibition in eating human blood, but the Sages forbade eating human blood because of mar'it ayin. Therefore, one who bites into bread or an apple, and blood from his mouth remains on them, must scrape the blood off the bread or the apple, and afterwards he may continue to eat them. However, blood that comes out from between the teeth may be sucked, since it has not left the body, and there is no prohibition at all in it. Moreover, a person may suck blood that comes out of a finger, since it is evident that it is from the finger and not from a domestic animal or a bird (SA 66:10; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 47).</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
